> One problem I noticed is that the spelling conflicts with https://whatwg.org/style-guide. We have popup as a non-hyphenated-all-lowercase word.

I believe "pop-up" was decided here: https://github.com/openui/open-ui/issues/546#issuecomment-1156988124

@domenic do you have any thoughts about the style guide dictionary?

Here's how I'd do it with view transitions:

https://simple-set-demos.glitch.me/dialog/faked/ - run in Chrome Canary with `chrome://flags/#view-transition` enabled.

https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/93594/202161274-d2b80c89-edc3-4acf-81cd-5a25c2d891f9.mp4

We've got a bug right now with view transitions and top-level stuff, so I've created a fake dialog outside the top-level for the sake of this demo.

<details>
<summary>Code</summary>

```js
const dialogContainer = document.querySelector('.fake-dialog-container');

document.querySelector('.open-dialog').addEventListener('click', () => {
  transition('show-dialog-transition', () => dialogContainer.classList.remove('hidden'));
});

document.querySelector('.close-dialog').addEventListener('click', () => {
  transition('hide-dialog-transition', () => dialogContainer.classList.add('hidden'));
});

async function transition(className, callback) {
  if (!document.startViewTransition) {
    callback();
    return;
  }
  
  try {
    document.documentElement.classList.add(className);
    await document.startViewTransition(callback).finished;  
  } finally {
    document.documentElement.classList.remove(className);
  }
}
```

```css
@keyframes show-dialog {
  from { transform: scale(0.5); }
}

@keyframes hide-dialog {
  to { transform: scale(0.5); }
}

@keyframes fade-in {
  from { opacity: 0; }
}

@keyframes fade-out {
  to { opacity: 0; }
}

.show-dialog-transition::view-transition-new(dialog) {
  animation:
    200ms ease-out both fade-in,
    300ms cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1) both show-dialog;
}

.hide-dialog-transition::view-transition-old(dialog) {
  animation:
    200ms ease-out both fade-out,
    300ms ease-out both hide-dialog;
}
```

</details>

- Adding and removing the class from the root element won't be necessary once we implement https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/8057
- This transition covers the whole document, so it would probably benefit from [scoped transitions](https://github.com/WICG/view-transitions/blob/main/scoped-transitions.md) so the transition is limited to the dialog/popover.

Although I had to 'fake' this demo due to the top-level bug, it does prove that this solution works for more than just top-level elements.

The 'dialog' is gone as soon as you press 'close', so it's impossible to double-press the close button etc. The DOM interaction becomes really simple for the developer.

For dialog, it would still involve capturing the usual close signals, cancelling them, and wrapping a 'real' close call in `startViewTransition`. I think we could avoid this by adding an integration for dialog/popover, where it automatically does this on show/hide. Either something like `dialog.showModal({ transition: true })`, or an attribute on the element.

Ok, just to simplify the discussion, and if I understood the demo correctly, here's just the code for your proposal:

<details>

<summary>Code</summary>

```html
<button onclick="d.showModal()">Open the Dialog</button>
<dialog startviewtransition="dialog-transition" id=d> The Dialog
  <button onclick="d.close()">Close</button>
</dialog>

<style>
dialog {
  view-transition-name: dialog-transition;
  /* This is required by View Transitions: */
  contain: paint;
}

/* The "other" endpoint opacity:1 is provided by View Transitions, right? */
@keyframes fade-in {
  from { opacity: 0; }
}

@keyframes fade-out {
  to { opacity: 0; }
}

::view-transition-new(dialog-transition) {
  animation: 500ms both fade-in;
}

::view-transition-old(dialog-transition) {
  animation: 500ms both fade-out;
}
</style>
```
</details>

I've made some assumptions above. One is that a new `startviewtransition` attribute causes `dialog.show()`, `dialog.showModal()`, **and** `dialog.close()` to all do the equivalent of:
```javascript
document.startViewTransition(() => {
  if (was_a_show_transition)
    dialog.show(); // Make the dialog visible and put it in the top layer
  else
    dialog.close(); // Hide the dialog and remove from the top layer
);
```

Some questions:
1. Am I correct in my minimized code above?
2. The `startViewTransition()` method doesn't take any names for parameters. What if there are multiple view transitions defined for a page - this starts them all?
3. (About your demo) the `::backdrop` element only seems to show up after the animation completes. I assume that's not intentional/correct?

Given the above, it seems like (with some work) View Transitions might be able to be the "general purpose" animation feature that will work on `<dialog>` and Popover, and even the general `display:none` element case. There are a few limitations that I see, relative to what's available in Popover:

1. You **can't use `transition`s** to create animations.
2. The developer-facing API is decently **more complex** than something simple like `transition: opacity 1s`.
5. You **can't have multiple animations** running at a time. This precludes e.g. nested Popover animations.
6. Conversely, it seems like this will automatically start all view transitions on the page, including ones **unrelated** to the dialog show/hide.
7. The dialog **isn't interactive until the animation is done**. If the developer has chosen a long animation timeout, the user won't be able to click the Close button until the View Transition finishes, even if everything is visible to them. This could cause user frustration.

It's also important to note that this will **still require customization for each API**. For example, the `startViewTransition()` call needs to be inserted into the appropriate place in all of the existing algorithms, delaying the show/hide algorithms until the `startViewTransition` callback is called. That will make some currently-synchronous things asynchronous, e.g. the [dialog focusing steps](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interactive-elements.html#dialog-focusing-steps), so it needs some per-API thought and care.

Given the above limitations, and given that this View Transitions approach would seem to still be completely compatible with the existing [Popover animation logic](https://open-ui.org/components/popup.research.explainer#animation-of-popovers), I'd say **let's do both!**. This can be the future path for more limited quick animations of single elements like dialog, while Popover can keep the more powerful and simpler animation API.

Perhaps we should branch off this discussion into a separate issue, perhaps on [CSSWG](https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues) or the [view-transitions repo](https://github.com/WICG/view-transitions/issues)?

> I guess in that case I'll just reiterate that I think we should pursue the general animation solution, and not include a second independent solution that's popover-specific. I think that will be a big mistake in terms of adding unnecessary complexity to the web platform and creating a confusing long-term API surface.

Hmm, well what do you think of my comments about the https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/8083 solution, which isn't "general" really. It needs special casing for all of the APIs (dialog, popover, div) that it might be used with, right? E.g. we'll need to decide, for each API, how to go about delaying the various things that usually happen immediately, during the animation. I think perhaps Popover is where we're exploring exactly what that might look like, in the case of a single API. No matter how you animate something from "showing" to "not showing" there will always be a "animating between" state, right? We just get to decide what it looks like.

If you're not talking about the implementation experience, and instead you're talking about the developer experience, I don't think Popover invents anything new at all. On the contrary, it does the work necessary to ensure existing animations APIs like the `animation` and `transition` CSS properties "just work" as they do everywhere else. I.e. I see this as the least confusing API surface I could think of:

```css
[popover] {
  opacity: 0;
  transition: opacity 0.5s;
}
[popover]:open {
  opacity: 1;
}
```

> There's a difference between "special casing" and "building on top of". We should for sure add nice entry points, like `showPopover({ viewTransition: true })`, which are syntactic sugar for a specific combination of view transitions + specific popover calls.

I really don't get this. The Popover proposal builds on top of [this](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/transition).

> But we should not have two separate types of "animating between" states on the web platforms: one for view transitions, and one for popovers.

Uh oh... [`transition`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/transition) and [ViewTransitions](https://www.w3.org/TR/css-view-transitions-1/) ...?

> I appreciate you think that API surface is not confusing. I think it's extremely confusing, given that it will side alongside view transitions CSS code which does "the same thing" for other elements.

I seriously don't understand the confusion. Can you clarify what will confuse developers about this code:

```css
[popover] {
  opacity: 0;
  transition: opacity 0.5s;
}
[popover]:open {
  opacity: 1;
}
```

That's how literally everything else on the web today is transitioned, modulo `<dialog>`. My fear is that **not** supporting the above **already** confuses and annoys and angers developers when they try to use it for `<dialog>`, and the argument seems to be that we can't support it for some reason. We can.

I’d also like to respond to the suggestion that we use a generic transition-to-display:none mechanism for popover exit animations. (aside: such a general mechanism for CSS is a great idea and a lot of developers have asked for it; that is tracked by Rob’s proposal [here](https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/6429)). If a popover or dialog transitioned only into the display:none state and there were no `:open` or `:closed` pseudo classes or top-layer involvement, then indeed we could use this mechanism.

But popovers allow the developer to close into a non-display:none state, also have these pseudo classes, and utilizes the top layer. So we need to keep it in the top layer during the animation regardless of destination style; if not, during the animation it would:
* Break ancestor clipping and z-index from its presence in the top layer
* Break developer styles that are designed to display it inline with other content after the animation completes

However, as the spec currently stands, :open is removed at the beginning of the popover dismiss animation, yet the popover remains in the top layer during the animation. Also, during the animation, neither `:open` nor `:closed` apply (@domenic mentioned this situation as well in a side chat). I agree these are a little complicated/inconsistent, so I propose the following:

* `:open` always applies when the popover is in the top layer. `:closed` always applies otherwise (change from current spec PR)
* Popover close animations run while the popover is in the top layer (no change from spec PR)
* During the close animation, a new pseudo class `:closing` is applied (change from spec PR). `:open` still applies (since the popover is still in the top layer). This allows developers and the UA to apply an exit animation by targeting `:closing` in their animation styles.
* During an animation to open a popover, the popover is in the top layer during the animation (and hence `:open` applies) (no change from spec PR).
* Add a `:willopen` pseudo class that only applies after the first style recalc of an open animation (the first one [here](https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/blink/renderer/core/html/html_element.cc;l=1305-1317;drc=41e0f1ec251a2a382aeb375e4fdae6a366e3390f)).

These changes should address the concerns above (about multiple states/inconsistency), while at the same time providing a cleaner way for developers to target the animation specifically.

This is all probably kind of confusing, so let’s look at a sample developer style for open and close animations. Open animation:

```
/* General baseline styles for the popover */
popover {
  display: flex;
  transition: opacity 150ms, transform 150ms; 
}

/* Closed state */
popover:closed {
  display:none; /* UA default style */
}

/* Open state */
popover:open {
  background: green;
}

/* Style indicating first frame of an opening animation */
popover:willopen {
  opacity: 0;
  transform: translateY(-50px);
}
```

What happens on open is:
1. UA fires the `popovershow` event (cancellable)
2. UA removes `:closed` and adds `:willopen` and `:open`, and places the popover in the top layer
3. UA forces style recalc (which has the effect of storing off the first animation keyframe)
4. UA removes `:willopen` (animations start here, if any)

Close:

```
/*  Style indicating last frame of a closing animation */
popover:closing {
  opacity: 0;
  transform: translateY(50px); /* Notice how it need not be in the same state as `:willopen` */
}
```

What happens:
1. UA forces style recalc and collects running animations
2. UA fires the `popoverhide` event (not cancellable)
3. UA adds `:closing`
4. UA forces style recalc, collects running animations, and diffs these animations from step 1
5. On completion of animations in step 4 (which run of their own accord), UA removes `:open` & `:closing`, removes the popover from the top layer, and adds `:closed`

> I have removed the animations integration from the PR

As mentioned above, we've decided to simplify the current popover spec PR by removing the animations stuff for now, while we figure out the best path forward to support popover animations. We'll likely open a fresh issue to tackle that subject, once we've had a chance to give it some thought.

@annevk
